//? Title: Lanczos resampling through actual window taps
//? Subtitle: Original signal, explicit normalization and negative-lobe limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Reconstructing a sampled signal between integer positions requires a rule for
weighting nearby observations and handling finite boundaries. Lanczos uses a
finite windowed-sinc kernel whose negative lobes can improve reconstruction on
some smooth signals while also causing ringing and phase-dependent raw gain. We
inspect actual tap identities/products, retain the original thirty-sample
signal, and compare raw and explicitly normalized modes. Neither a finite window
nor a good example supplies a universal accuracy or antialiasing guarantee.
)

\toc

> A finite window is a specific reconstruction rule

For positive integer half-width a, define

$$ L_a(x)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{sinc}(x)\operatorname{sinc}(x/a),&|x|<a,\\0,&|x|\ge a,\end{cases}\qquad \operatorname{sinc}(x)=\frac{\sin(\pi x)}{\pi x}. $$
\alttext(The Lanczos kernel is a product of two sinc factors inside a finite open support, with sinc of zero defined as one.)

For a query p, the native producer clamps its position to[0,n−1], then visits
virtual lattice taps floor(p)−a+1 through floor(p)+a in ascending order. Lookup
indices are separately clamped to border samples, retaining edge extension near
the boundary. The raw result sums sample*weight without dividing by the total
weight, matching the historical interior formula. Kernel weights need not sum
exactly one at a fractional phase, so constant samples can have phase-dependent
raw gain. Normalization is a separate explicit mode using the actual weight sum.

The mathematical kernel is cardinal at integer positions. The native
implementation now explicitly returns zero for nonzero exactly integral sinc
arguments, avoiding sin(pi*k) remnants and preserving numeric integer-sample
equality. This and safe clamping of the query correct the old outside-position
comment/implementation mismatch and unsafe empty/nonfinite casts; ordinary
interior noninteger arithmetic remains the same finite-window formula.

> See one actual six-tap query

Use seven samples0,1,4,9,16,25,36 and p2.5 with a3. The trace records each
actual virtual/stored identity, weight, product and accumulation boundary. The
diagram summarizes producing raw sum and actual weight sum; it does not generate
a second convolution.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lanczos_resampling_taps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t =
        dm::algorithms::lanczos_resample_trace({0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36}, 2.5, 3);
    std::print("| actual virtual tap | stored sample identity | actual sample | actual raw weight | actual " "weighted value | actual sum after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.taps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n",
                   e.virtual_index, e.sample_index, e.sample, e.weight,
                   e.weighted_value, e.sum_after);
    const auto &a = t.analysis;
    std::print("\nActual raw sum {:.12f}; actual weight sum {:.12f}; negative weights {}; clamped lookups " "{}.\n",
               a.raw_sum, a.weight_sum, a.work.negative_weights,
               a.work.clamped_taps);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("taps", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual taps {}\nquery {}", a.work.taps,
                       a.effective_position)).node("sum", {6, 0},
                                                   std::format(
                                                   "Actual raw value\n{:.8f}",
                                                   a.raw_sum)).node(
        "weights", {12, 0},
        std::format("Actual weight sum\n{:.8f}", a.weight_sum)).edge("taps",
                                                                     "sum").edge("sum", "weights").save_svg("build/lanczos_resampling_taps.svg");
}
```
